{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380356,"nonce":"bYnyLma1NfRwN3O5ag2cicjSm5s=","title":"drop_insert: a phi with a null incoming owns nothing, so what reaches it beside the null leaks","message":"`release_owned_phis` lets a phi own its storage only when every\nincoming is an owned site or another owning phi. A null constant fails\nthat test, so the merge of an allocation with the null an error path\nhands on in its place (`acc = phi [Vec3$new(...), body], [null,\nraised]`) is never released, and neither is anything it hands on to:\nthe loop accumulator of `bench_op_overload_ref.py` after nullable\ninstances made the exception path a null pointer rather than a box,\npeak RSS 179 MB for one million iterations.\n\nThe same shape already passes `functions_returning_owned_storage`,\nwhere a returned null counts as \"returns nothing\". A null incoming is\nthe same: nothing to release, and every release the pass emits\n(`intrinsic.free`, `zyntax_box_free`, `$IO$string_free`) is a no-op on\nnull. The fix skips null incomings (a `Null` constant or a zero of\npointer type) both when deciding ownership and when agreeing the\nrelease.\n\nA second leak in the same program: `zb_unbox_instance_raw` linked to\n`zyntax_box_data`, whose result the pass treats as another name for the\nbox (right for a string or opaque payload the box owns). The unboxed\ninstance address flowed into a phi that could not be owned, so the box\nholding it could not be either. `zyntax_box_pointer` is the reader for\na `zyntax_box_ptr` box, whose pointer the box does not own; the\ninstance, list, tuple and function readers use it, `zb_box_get_str`\nstays on `zyntax_box_data`.\n\nAfter both: op_overload_ref 52 MB, the same as a kernel that allocates\nnothing per iteration.","files":null}]}